---
import DemoGrid from '../components/DemoGrid.astro';
import type { OutlineEntry } from '../components/outline-entry';
import ResultsTable from '../components/ResultsTable.astro';
import type { DebatePart } from '../config/debate-part';
import { debateParts } from '../config/debate-parts';
import { togetherResults } from '../config/together-results';
import PartLayout from '../layouts/PartLayout.astro';

const part: DebatePart | undefined = debateParts.find((candidate: DebatePart): boolean => candidate.path === '/Together');

if (part === undefined) {
    throw new Error('src/config/debate-parts.ts has no part at /Together.');
}

const outline: ReadonlyArray<OutlineEntry> = [
    { id: 'memory', text: 'Memory' },
    { id: 'counting', text: 'Counting' },
    { id: 'demos', text: 'The demos' },
    { id: 'limit', text: 'The limit' },
    { id: 'score', text: 'How they score together' },
];

const elements: ReadonlyArray<string> = ['<details>', '<dialog>', '<datalist>', '<meter>', '<progress>', '<output>', '<template>', '<search>'];

const switchExample: string = `.bulb {
    filter: grayscale(1);
}

.room:has(#light:checked) .bulb {
    filter: none;
}`;

const countingExample: string = `.quiz { counter-reset: used; }
.quiz input:checked { counter-increment: used; }

.result::before {
    content: "You've used " counter(used) " of 8.";
}`;
---
<PartLayout part={part} outline={outline}>
    <p>
        <a href="/CSS">Part 3</a> ended on a mismatch. HTML can hold state, such as a ticked box or an open section, but
        can't make decisions about it. CSS can make decisions all day, but has no state of its own. Put them together
        and each fills the other's gap: HTML remembers, and CSS decides.
    </p>

    <h2 id="memory">Memory</h2>
    <p>
        A checkbox is a single bit of memory: it's either ticked or it isn't, and it stays that way until someone
        changes it. A group of radio buttons is a little more: one choice out of several, like a variable that can hold
        one of a fixed set of values. Labels let any part of the page flip them.
    </p>
    <p>
        CSS reads that memory with <code>:checked</code>. Until recently it could only react in elements that came
        after the checkbox; <code>:has()</code> removed that limit, so now any part of a page can respond to any box
        on it. Here's the simplest possible example, a light switch:
    </p>

    <div class="demo surface room">
        <label class="switch">
            <input class="switch-input" type="checkbox" id="light" role="switch" />
            <span class="plate" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rocker"></span></span>
            <span class="switch-name">Light switch</span>
        </label>
        <div class="light">
            <span class="bulb" aria-hidden="true">💡</span>
            <p class="lamp-state"></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p>
        The switch is a checkbox in disguise: the box is hidden inside its label, and the label is drawn to look like a
        switch. Clicking the label ticks the box, and CSS does the rest:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{switchExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        One bit of memory in HTML, one decision in CSS. Everything that follows is built from more of the same.
    </p>

    <h2 id="counting">Counting</h2>
    <p>
        CSS counters, which <a href="/CSS#calculating">part 3</a> used to print the results of sums, can also count
        ticked boxes, because a counter can go up only on elements that match a condition. Which of these HTML elements
        have you used?
    </p>

    <div class="demo surface quiz">
        <ul class="quiz-options" role="list">
            {elements.map((element: string, index: number) => (
                <li>
                    <label>
                        <input type="checkbox" id={`element-${index}`} />
                        <code>{element}</code>
                    </label>
                </li>
            ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="result"></p>
    </div>

    <pre><code>{countingExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        That's input, memory, a calculation and output, with no script anywhere. Ben Romy wrote that HTML "can't take
        input and produce output". On its own, no; with CSS, it just did.
    </p>

    <h2 id="demos">The demos</h2>
    <p>
        The demos push the same ideas further. Each one has its own page, with an explanation of how it works and the
        one thing it proves.
    </p>

    <DemoGrid />

    <p>All the demos are also listed on the <a href="/Demos">demos page</a>, linked from the top of every page.</p>

    <h2 id="limit">The limit: someone has to keep clicking</h2>
    <p>
        So can HTML and CSS together compute anything? Surprisingly, there's a sense in which they can. Rule 110 is a
        row of cells, each on or off, where every new row follows from the one above by a single simple rule. In 2004
        the mathematician Matthew Cook published a proof that Rule 110 can compute anything a computer can: it's
        Turing-complete. And Rule 110 has been built in HTML and CSS, notably by Eli Fox-Epstein in 2011; there's a
        version you can <a href="/Demos/Rule-110">try in the demos</a>.
    </p>
    <p>That makes HTML and CSS Turing-complete too, with two catches:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The page must be big enough.</strong> Every cell has to exist in the HTML before the page loads, so
            the size of the computation is fixed in advance. That's a lesser objection than it sounds: every real
            computer has a fixed amount of memory too.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A person has to keep clicking.</strong> CSS can work out what the next row should be, but it can't
            make it happen. Someone has to click to move the machine on, every step, forever. The person is the clock.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The second catch is the real one, and it shows up in a simpler way too. HTML and CSS are very good at
        <em>checking</em> an answer: is this game won, is this sum right, is this a valid move? They can't
        <em>search</em> for one, because searching means trying something, remembering the result, undoing it and
        trying again, over and over, and only the person clicking can do that. A <a href="/Demos/Knights-Tour">knight's tour</a> is easy to
        check in CSS. Finding one, CSS would never do by itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="score">How they score together</h2>
    <p>Against the four tests, HTML and CSS together come out like this:</p>

    <ResultsTable subject="HTML and CSS" results={togetherResults} />

    <p>
        That's a very different picture from either on its own. Together they pass the middle test outright, and get
        further on the strictest than most people would guess. Whether that's enough to call them a programming
        language is what the <a href="/Verdict">verdict</a> is about.
    </p>
</PartLayout>

<style>
    .demo {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1rem;
        padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    }

    .demo > :global(*) {
        margin-top: 0;
    }

    .demo input[type='checkbox'] {
        width: 1.2rem;
        height: 1.2rem;
        accent-color: var(--brand-blue);
    }

    /*
     * The light switch: a checkbox hidden inside its label, with the label drawn as a wall plate and rocker. The box stays
     * in the page, out of sight, so it can still be reached with the keyboard and is announced as a switch.
     */
    .room {
        display: flex;
        align-items: stretch;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* The switch and the light are laid out alike, picture above caption, so the two captions line up. */
    .switch,
    .light {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
        justify-items: center;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .switch {
        cursor: pointer;
    }

    .room input.switch-input {
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        opacity: 0;
    }

    /* The fascia: a raised plate holding the rocker, with a screw either side. */
    .plate {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 1rem;
        padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        corner-shape: var(--corner-shape);
        background: var(--surface-raised);
        box-shadow: var(--edge-shadow), var(--shadow-md);
        --edge-top: var(--edge-light);
        --edge-bottom: var(--edge-dark);
    }

    .plate::before,
    .plate::after {
        content: '';
        width: 0.6rem;
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        border-radius: 50%;
        corner-shape: round;
        background: var(--border-strong);
    }

    /* The rocker: the pressed half is in shadow. Off, the top is pressed in; on, the bottom. */
    .rocker {
        width: 1.9rem;
        height: 3.2rem;
        border-radius: 0.45rem;
        background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--surface-alt) 0 50%, var(--surface) 50% 100%);
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--border-strong), 0 0.15rem 0.3rem rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
    }

    .switch-input:checked + .plate .rocker {
        background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--surface) 0 50%, var(--surface-alt) 50% 100%);
    }

    .switch-input:focus-visible + .plate {
        outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
        outline-offset: 3px;
    }

    /* The switch's caption and the light's state share one style. */
    .switch-name,
    .lamp-state {
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .bulb {
        font-size: 4.5rem;
        line-height: 1;
        filter: grayscale(1) opacity(0.45);
        transition: filter 300ms;
    }

    .room:has(#light:checked) .bulb {
        filter: drop-shadow(0 0 1.25rem color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-amber) 80%, transparent));
    }

    .lamp-state::before {
        content: 'The light is off.';
    }

    .room:has(#light:checked) .lamp-state::before {
        content: 'The light is on.';
    }

    /* The counting quiz: a counter that only ticked boxes add to, printed after them. */
    .quiz {
        counter-reset: used;
    }

    .quiz-options {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(9.5rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.5rem;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .quiz-options li {
        margin: 0;
    }

    .quiz-options label {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.6rem;
        padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        cursor: pointer;
    }

    .quiz-options code {
        padding: 0;
        background: none;
    }

    .quiz input:checked {
        counter-increment: used;
    }

    .result {
        color: var(--heading);
        font-size: var(--text-lg);
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .result::before {
        content: "You've used " counter(used) ' of 8.';
    }

    .quiz:not(:has(input:not(:checked))) .result::after {
        content: ' Full marks.';
        color: var(--success);
    }
</style>
